A||N||5||Taijiguy||Dash removal||||1||
Z||000000||Taijiguy||04-03-2001||12:46 PM||||Just came up with (what *I* think) is a pretty cool custom dash design. Never completely pulled the dash out of one of these before. What all is involved? Do I have to yank the HVAC housing, and all that hoorah, or is it a pretty simple matter (in relative terms anyway) Any advice of course is greatly appreciated.<BR>I'm goinna have to quit working so I have time to do all this stuff to my Fiero....friggin work always gets in the way of FUN!<BR>~Taiji||||reg||1
Z||000001||mrfixit58||04-03-2001||02:19 PM||||Tia,<P>The good news is it's all a bolt OUT bolt IN procedure. The bad news is it involves removing just about EVERY bolt!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"> You'll need to remove the center console, the radio console, loosen the console frame, remove the speedo/tach gauge pod, remove the front  speakers, remove the bolts from the bottom corner on each side of the dash and the four(?) screws along the top. Whew! <P>Good Luck,<BR>Roy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000002||GoCartGuy||04-03-2001||08:56 PM||||OK, the in and out is simple, of coarse there is quite a few screws/bolts.  The other thing to be wary of is around the front center console just above the AC/Heat controls.  The plastic surround that this and the radio fit into has two plastic prongs that stick into the dash to help hold it in there.  This part (the plastic) is flexible to a point, but removing it would be a serious pain.  Just be very careful when you loosen these two prongs from the dash, if they break the dash won't sit down well in the center.<P>Best of luck, send some pics.<P>BTW, I've pulled two dashes and put in one...whole lotta screws.<P>------------------<BR>'85 GT "The GoCart"||||reg||1
Z||000003||Taijiguy||04-03-2001||09:06 PM||||I appreciate the tip on those, since I won't be reinstalling it, but I will be selling it...gray, with nearly perfect corners(tiny unnoticable ding on drivers side) No cracks or damage of any other sort.(aside from paint)The radio/cc pod has typical top damage, but the console support is also perfect. These are commodities I think  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||ToddAsh||04-03-2001||09:14 PM|||| Took the dash out of my '84 last night. Not too bad. Didn't take out console or gauge pod.Left speakers in. Removed 5mm bolts along bottom of dash,took of radio trim panel and removed bolts,popped off speaker grills and removed 2 bolts on each side and wiggled that baby out.Dropped column tonight but haven't figured out how to disconnect shifter lockout cable. Good Luck,Todd||||reg||1
Z||000005||GoCartGuy||04-04-2001||01:50 AM||||Ok on repairs to your almost perfect dash.  For the little ding on the corner, you can get a vinyl/leather repair kit...works nice most auto parts stores have them, something like $8 or so.  For the damage to your plastic console around the radio/AC section and also for major corner repairs, pick up NAPA's Flex Bumper repair kit, cost me like $25 but it works great.  Its also good for rebacking those holes in the plastic where you tighten to torx screw too much.<P>------------------<BR>'85 GT "The GoCart"||||reg||1
